rly without sufficient justification and risks being harassed by pawns.\n- P: b2-b4 or P: a2-a4 – Flank moves that are less effective at this stage; not contributing to central control or rapid development.\n- N: b1-c3 – Good developing move, supporting d4 and preparing for harmonious development.\n\nReason: The most principled approach here is to either develop a knight to a strong central square or solidify the center with d2-d4. Among these, N: g1-f3 is one of the most common and effective opening moves — it develops a piece, supports central control, avoids blocking the d-pawn, and keeps options open for castling. It also prepares for quick kingside development and potential support of an e5 push or defense of e4.\n\nAnswer: N: g1-f3"}
