\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|QWF0|KWF0|BWF0|    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, Q: d1-e2, P: d2-d4, P: d2-d3, K: e1-e2, P: e5-e6, B: f1-e2, B: f1-d3, B: f1-c4, B: f1-b5, B: f1-a6, N: f3-d4, N: f3-g1, N: f3-g5, N: f3-h4, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, R: h1-g1, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in an early-mid game state. Black has a pawn on c7 that advanced to c5 (now at c6), and another on e5 that pushed to e5 from e7 (currently at e5). My pawn on e5 is attacking d6 and f6, but no immediate captures are possible. Several of my pieces have limited mobility due to central congestion. The knight on f3 is active but could be repositioned for greater influence. Black’s kingside is slightly weakened with the absence of a knight on g8 and a missing bishop on g7 — though not visible here, this suggests potential vulnerability. My king is still in the center but has not moved (KWF0), so castling rights are intact, though not immediately executable due to lack of rook coordination.\n\nBlack just played ...c7-c6 (likely after capturing or advancing), and their e-pawn is now on e5. My pawn on e5 is pinned potentially only if the queen or king is exposed, but currently it's stable. However, my light-squared bishop on f1 is blocked in by pawns on e2 and d3 (not present), so developing it via Bf1-e2 or Bf1-d3 is useful. Also, the knight on f3 can jump to d4, pressuring e6 or c5, or support central control.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: e5-e6 – This pawn push would attack the black king area (if castled) and open lines for development. It threatens to promote if supported and may force Black to respond immediately (e.g., f7-f6 or Nxg5). Strong candidate.\n- N: f3-d4 – Centralizes the knight, attacks c6 and e6, increases pressure on Black’s center. Solid developing move.\n- B: f1-e2 – Safe development, prepares kingside castling, connects rooks eventually. Passive but sound.\n- B: f1-c4 – Aggressive; targets f7 weakness (classic), eyes the weak square f7. Very strong idea in many openings.\n- P: d2-d4 – Opens the center, fights for space. But may block the bishop on c1 if not careful. Premature without preparation?\n- Q: d1-e2 – Connects with kingside, supports e5 pawn, prepares castling. Reasonable, but queen may be slightly exposed later.\n\nReason: Among these, **B: f1-c4** stands out. It develops a piece with tempo and threat: the bishop targets f7, which is a known weak point in Black’s camp (only defended by the king). If Black isn’t careful, this could lead to tactics like Bxf7+ followed by knight involvement. Additionally, it doesn't block any central pawns and improves my long-term activity. While e5-e6 is tempting, it might allow counterplay with ...d6xd5 or weaken my own structure if unsupported. In contrast, Bc4 is principled, aggressive, and aligns with opening principles.\n\nAnswer: B: f1-c4"}
