ecting the rooks. A safe and principled move.\n\nAmong these, N: f3-d4 stands out as it occupies the strong central square d4, pressures Black’s structure, and develops actively without wasting time. It doesn’t allow Black to easily counter and increases control over key squares.\n\nReason: The knight move to d4 is both developmental and disruptive, increasing pressure in the center and preparing for potential attacks or structural advantages. It is more dynamic than passive development or pawn pushes that might be met with counterplay.\n\nAnswer: N: f3-d4"}
