awn — bad.\n\nSame with knight.\n\nBut perhaps for initiative.\n\nBetter idea: **B: g5-e7** — bishop to e7.\n\ne7 is behind Black’s lines. Is it occupied? Row 7: b7 (BBF1), e7 is empty? Board says: row 7: a7 empty, b7 BBF1, c7 empty, d7 empty, e7 empty, f7 empty, g7 BBF1, h7 PBF0.\n\nSo e7 is empty.\n\nBishop from g5 to e7 — that’s two left, two up — not diagonal. Invalid.\n\ng5 to e7 is file change -2, rank +2 — that’s diagonal (southeast to northwest). Yes — same color square? Assuming g5 is light, e7 is light — yes.\n\nSo **B: g5-e7** is a valid move — long diagonal.\n\nDoes it attack anything? From e7, bishop points toward d8, c9… but d8 has nothing. a4? No.\n\nNot useful.\n\nWait — **B: g5-f4** — f4 is empty. From f4, bishop eyes e5 — which is occupied by Black’s king-side pawn protecting the king.\n\nIf I play Bg5-f4, I threaten the e5 pawn. If Black doesn’t respond, I can take it next move — Bxe5+, checking the king on f5.\n\nYes!\n\nBecause e5 is diagonally adjacent to f4 — so **B: g5-f4** prepares **Bxe5+** next move.\n\nAnd after Bxe5+, Black must recapture — with king taking back to e5 or f5–e5? King on f5 can take on e5? f5 to e5 is horizontal — yes, but only if not pinned.\n\nBut Bxe5+ checks the king — because bishop on e5 attacks f5 along diagonal? e5 to f5 is horizontal — not diagonal.\n\nOh! Mistake.\n\nBishop on f4 attacks e5 — yes, diagonally (f4 to e5 is one up-left — diagonal).\n\nBut if I capture on e5 — Bxe5 — then the bishop is on e5.\n\nNow, does e5 see f5? e5 and f5 are adjacent horizontally — so a rook or queen could attack, but **a bishop cannot** attack horizontally.\n\nSo **Bxe5 does NOT check the king on f5**.\n\nIt just captures the pawn.\n\nThen Black can play normally — maybe push the king or develop.\n\nBut not a direct threat.\n\nHowever, removing the e5 pawn might weaken the king.\n\nBut better idea: **N: c3-b5**? Not in list.\n\nWait — what about **N: c3-e4**?\n\ne4 is empty. From e4, knight can go to d6, f6, g5, g3, etc.\n\nBut