 on g4**, and it\'s surrounded by minimal protection. This is extraordinary — the black king is stranded in the center, likely due to castling never having occurred and poor development.\n\nThis changes everything. The black king is wide open. I have a knight on f5 (NWF1), which attacks **g7, e7, d6, h6, h4, g3, e3, d4** — but not directly g4. However, **h4** is adjacent to g4, and **f5-h4** is one of my valid moves.\n\nAlso, my bishop on c6 (BWF1) controls the a4–e8 diagonal. Can it attack near g4? From c6, it goes through d5, e4, f3, g2 — not useful for g4. But if I move a piece to h3 or h4, maybe I can coordinate.\n\nWait — **P: g2-g3** is a valid move. That prepares Bh3 or Nh3. But better: **N: f5-h4** is available. That puts the knight adjacent to the black king on g4 — checks it? Let’s see: from h4 to g4 is one square left — yes, knight on h4 attacks g4.\n\nSo Nf5-h4+ delivers **check**!\n\nAnd since the black king is on g4, with limited escape squares:\n- g4 escapes: f4 (attacked?), h4 (occupied), g5 (empty?), f3, h3, f5, h5 — but many are off board or occupied.\n\nBut crucially, after Nf5-h4+, the king must move. Possible escapes:\n- g4-f4? Is f4 attacked? Bishop on c6 doesn’t hit it. But pawns or other pieces?\n- g4-h5? Possible.\n- g4-f3? Unlikely, probably not safe.\n\nBut more importantly: **after Nxh4+, if Black recaptures... but wait, it\'s a check — they must respond.**\n\nBut even better: **is g4 under any other attack?**\n\nMy pawn on f4? No — I have a pawn on f2? Wait: f2 has no pawn. On f2: nothing. f3: no piece. But I have a pawn on f2? No — looking at row 2: f2 is empty. But I have PWT1 on f4? Yes! **PWT1 on f4** — so pawn on f4, which is on f4.\n\nf4 is one square below g4 — so f4 pawn attacks g5 and e5, not g4.\n\nBut knight on h4 checks g4 directly.\n\nSo **N: f5-h4** gives check — and this is a massive tactical blow.\n\nIs it in the valid moves? Yes: **N: f5-h4** is listed.\n\nNow, what happens after Nf5-h4+?\n\nBlack king must move from g4. Where can it go?\n\n- g4–g5: possible, but then I can play Nf5xh4 or follow up with Bc6-e4+ or Qd1-h5#? Wait — do I have a queen?\n\nI haven\'t moved my queen yet. It’s still at home. But I don’t need her.\n\nAfter 1. Nf5-h4+ Kg5\n\nThen 2. Be4+! (bishop from c6 to e4) attacks g5 — and it\'s a discovered check potential? Or just a direct check.\n\nFrom c6 to e4 — yes, Be4+! Then king must go to h6 or g6 — and then Ng6 or h5+ etc., leading to mate.\n\nAlternatively, after Nf5-h4+ Kg5, I play h3! or even h4+.\n\nBut even better: after Nf5-h4+ Kg5, I can play **Ng6!** — knight from h4 to g6? No, h4 to g6 is not a knight move.\n\nh4 to g6 is L-shaped? 1 down, 2 left — yes, that’s a knight move.\n\nBut let\'s not get ahead.\n\nThe key point: **Nf5-h4+ is a devastating check that exploits the exposed black king.**\n\nAre there alternatives?\n\n- Could I play something else like Bc6-e4 immediately? But Be4 does not give check, and black could play ...Kg5 or even ...Kh5, trying to escape.\n\nBut after **Nf5-h4+**, the check forces the king to react immediately.\n\nOther moves like P: a2-a4 or R: a1-b1 are slow and ignore the tactical opportunity.\n\nEven developing queenside is irrelevant — this is a "kill the king" moment.\n\nSo **N: f5-h4** is the strongest move.\n\nIt’s in the valid list: **N: f5-h4**\n\nNo promotion needed — it\'s a knight move.\n\nEvaluation:\n- N: f5-h4 — delivers check, attacks king directly, forces weakening moves, likely leads to mate or material gain.\n- B: c6-e4 — targets f5 or future g5, but slower; black king escapes.\n- P: g2-g3 — prepares Bh3, but too slow.\n- R: f1-f2 — passive.\n- N: f5-d6 — attacks queen? But black queen on b7 — Nd6 might threaten, but ignores king.\n\nNone compare to the immediacy of **Nf5-h4+**.\n\nReason: The black king is dangerously exposed on g4. Nf5-h4+ delivers a check that disrupts Black’s position, limits king mobility, and opens the door for a quick mating attack, possibly involving the bishop on c6 and other pieces. This is the most forcing and advantageous move.\n\nAnswer: N: f5-h4'}
