d or losing the king.\n\nBut let\'s confirm legality: How did the black king get to g4? Regardless, per the board state, it\'s there — and that gives me a huge tactical advantage.\n\nNow, evaluating immediate threats and opportunities:\n\n- My knight on f5 (NWF1) is one knight-hop away from delivering check: Nf5-h4+ attacks g2, but more importantly, Nf5-h6+ would attack g4 — and h6 is adjacent to g4 diagonally, but knight moves from f5 to h6 is valid (f5 → h6: two up, one right — yes), and h6 attacks g4? No: knight on h6 attacks g8, f7, e6, f5, g4? Let\'s compute:\n\nKnight on f5:\n- d4, d6, e3, e7, g3, g7, h4, h6\n\nFrom h6, knight attacks: g8, f7, e6, g4? No — g4 is two files left and two ranks down — not an L-shape. So Nf5-h6 does **not** attack g4.\n\nWhat square would attack g4?\n\n- Any piece on f6, h6, f4, h4, e5, e3, etc.? Knight from f5 to g3? g3 is on the list — Nf5-g3 goes to g3, which is adjacent to g4 — no, doesn’t attack it.\n\nWait: what **does** attack g4?\n\nLet’s see:\n\n- Bishop on c6: c6 to g4 is along the diagonal (c6 → d5 → e4 → f3 → g2 → h1)? No — that’s the wrong diagonal. c6 is light-squared (c-file, 6th rank), moving to b5, a4, d5, e4, f3, g2, h1 — so yes! Bishop on c6 **does** attack g2, but not g4. g4 is two files right and two ranks down — not aligned.\n\nWait: c6 to g4: delta file +4, delta rank -2 — not diagonal. So no.\n\nHow about knight on f5 to e3? e3 is not near g4.\n\nWait — is anything attacking g4?\n\nLet’s consider: my rook on f1 — if I play Rf1-f4, that would attack g4? f4 is same rank as g4? No — f1 to f4 is vertical. f4 is on rank 4, file f. From f4, rook attacks all of rank 4 — including g4! Yes!\n\nSo if I play Rf1-f4, then rook checks the black king on g4.\n\nBut is that a legal move? Is Rf1-f4 in the valid moves list?\n\nValid moves: R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, R: f1-c1, R: f1-b1, R: f1-f2, R: f1-f3 — but **no R: f1-f4**.\n\nWhy not? Because from f1 to f4, it must pass through f2 and f3 — but is anything blocking?\n\nCurrent board:\n\n- f1: Rook\n- f2: PWF0 (white pawn)\n- f3: empty?\n- f4: KBF1 (black king)\n\nWait — f2 has a white pawn (PWF0), so the rook on f1 cannot go to f4 because the pawn is on f2 blocking the path.\n\nSimilarly, the rook cannot jump over the pawn.\n\nSo Rf1-f4 is not possible.\n\nIs there any piece that can deliver check to the black king on g4?\n\nCheck means the king is under attack.\n\nCurrently, is the black king on g4 in check? From the position, no piece is attacking g4 — otherwise it would be illegal for Black to leave their king in check.\n\nSo currently, KBF1 on g4 is **not** in check — but it\'s extremely exposed.\n\nNow, can I deliver check on the next move?\n\nLet’s analyze:\n\n- Can my knight on f5 go to h4? h4 is on the list: N: f5-h4 — yes.\n\nFrom h4, knight attacks: g6, f5, f3, g2, i3? No — standard knight moves: h4 attacks g6, f5, f3, g2? Wait:\n\nKnight on h4:\n- g6 (+1 file, +2 rank)\n- f5 (+2 file? no — h to f is -2, 4 to 5 is +1 → yes, f5)\n- f3 (-2 files, -1 rank) → f3\n- g2 (-1 file, -2 rank) → g2\n- i2? No\n- i6? No\n\nSo h4 attacks f3, f5, g6, g2 — **not g4**.\n\ng4 is adjacent — no.\n\nWhat about Nf5-g3? g3 is allowed (N: f5-g3).\n\nFrom g3, knight attacks: h5, f5, e4, e2, f1, h1.\n\ne4? e4 is empty. But g4? No — not attacked.\n\nNo knight move from f5 directly attacks g4.\n\nBishop on c6: possible moves include e4? B: c6-e4 is in the list.\n\nFrom e4, bishop attacks along diagonals: e4 to f5, g6, h7; d5, c6, b7, a8; f3, g2, h1; d3, c2, b1.\n\nDoes it attack g4? e4 to g4 is +2 files, 0 ranks — horizontal, not diagonal. So no.\n\nRooks: only way to attack g4 is from same rank (rank 4) or same file (g-file).\n\nOn rank 4: we have KBF1 (g4), PWF1 (f4), and others?\n\nBoard: f4 has PWF1 (white pawn), g4 has black king, h4 empty.\n\nSo if I had a rook on f4, it would attack g4 — but I don’t.\n\nOn g-file: g1 has KWF1, g2 has PWF0, g3 empty, g4 has black king.\n\nSo if I play rook to g3 or higher — but I don’t have a rook on g-file.\n\nMy rook