 Connects rooks? But rooks aren\'t connected yet; premature.\n- N: f5-d6 – Knight retreat? No, this is Black’s knight — wait, no! **f5 is occupied by Black knight (NBF1)** — so **N: f5-d4** etc. are **Black’s possible moves**, not mine! Wait — critical error!\n\nWait: Re-examining the list: the valid moves provided include **N: f5-d4**, but f5 has a **black knight (NBF1)**. So those "N: f5-*" moves must be **Black\'s moves**, but they are listed in *my* valid moves? That can\'t be.\n\nAh — this is a parsing issue. Looking again:\n\nThe valid moves are:\n...\nN: f5-d4, N: f5-d6, ... — these are **not** White’s moves — they are **Black’s knight moves** — but they are listed in *White’s* valid move list? That’s impossible.\n\nTherefore, this must be a **labeling error** in interpretation.\n\nBut the instruction says: “Your valid moves are...” — so only moves for White.\n\nThus, **N: f5-d4** cannot be a valid move for White — because there is no White knight on f5.\n\nLooking at the board: On f5 is **KBF1** — that’s the **Black King**?\n\nWait — let’s recheck piece encoding.\n\nEncoding: [Type][Color][2Step][Moved]\n\nSo f5 has: KBF1 → Type=K, Color=B → **Black King** on f5?\n\nThat can’t be right — kings don’t go to f5 early.\n\nWait — no: row 5:  \na5: PBF1 (Black pawn)  \nc5: PBF1 (Black pawn)  \nf5: NWF1 → Ah! There it is: **NWF1** = **Knight, White, Moved, NOT two-step**\n\nYes! f5: NWF1 → **White knight on f5**\n\nSimilarly, g5: KBF1 → Black King? On g5? In the middle of the board?\n\nWait — g5: KBF1 → Black King on g5? That would be extremely unusual.\n\nBut looking at the board:\n\nRow 5: ... |    |NWF1|KBF1|BWF1| 5\n\nSo f5: NWF1 (White knight)  \ng5: KBF1 (Black King?)  \nh5: BWF1 (White bishop)\n\nBut Black king on g5? That would mean Black king has moved into the open board — highly unlikely unless it\'s a handicap game.\n\nBut let’s check other king: White king is on g1: KWF1 — yes, standard.\n\nIs there another king? No — only one per side.\n\nSo **KBF1 on g5** must be the **Black King**.\n\nThat means Black king has already moved and is dangerously exposed in the center.\n\nThis changes everything.\n\nSo current position: **Black King is on g5**, having moved (KBF1), likely due to castling being impossible or lost.\n\nWhite has a **knight on f5 (NWF1)** — which is **attacking the square e7, g7, h6, d4, d6, e3, g3, h4**, but most importantly — **the knight on f5 is checking the Black King on g5**?\n\nLet’s see: Knight on f5, King on g5 — distance: same rank, one file apart — not a knight move.\n\nKnight moves: ±2 files and ±1 rank, or ±1 file and ±2 ranks.\n\nFrom f5: \n- e7 (+1 file, +2 ranks): no\n- g7 (+1 file, +2): g7 is empty\n- h6 (+2, +1): h6 empty\n- h4 (+2, -1): h4 empty\n- g3 (+1, -2): g3 empty\n- e3 (-1, -2): e3 empty\n- d4 (-2, -1): d4 empty\n- d6 (-2, +1): d6 has NBF1 (Black knight)\n\nSo **no, the White knight on f5 is NOT checking the Black king on g5** — it\'s too far.\n\nBut now, what about the **bishop on h5 (BWF1)**? It’s on h5, color white.\n\nIt attacks along the diagonal: g4, f3, e2, d1 — and also g6, f7, e8.\n\nBlack king is on g5 — is it in the line of fire?\n\nFrom h5 to g5: adjacent — not diagonal. So no.\n\nBut **if we play B: h5-g6**, then the bishop moves to g6 — which is diagonally adjacent to g5? No: g6 to g5 is vertical.\n\nDiagonals from g6: f5, f7, h5, h7 — none is g5.\n\nWait: **from h5 to g6** is one square diagonally down-left — yes: h5 → g6 is a valid diagonal move.\n\nNow, from g6, is the Black king on g5 under attack?\n\ng6 and g5 are on the same file — not diagonal. So no direct attack.\n\nBut what if the bishop goes to **f7**? From h5 to f7: h5 → g6 → f7 — two-square diagonal.\n\nf7: currently empty.\n\nFrom f7, the diagonal to g5? f7 to g5: +1 file, -2 ranks — not diagonal. Diagonal would be +1,-1 or -1,-1 etc.\n\nSo no.\n\nBut wait: **What if we play B: h5-f3**?\n\nFrom h5 to f3: down 2 ranks, left 2 files — diagonal? Yes: h5-g4-f3.\n\nf3 is empty.\n\nNow, does f3 attack g5