\n1 |RWF0|    |    |    |    |    |KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, R: a1-d1, R: a1-e1, R: a1-f1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-b3, P: a2-a3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-b3, P: c2-c3, N: e3-c4, N: e3-d1, N: e3-d5, N: e3-f1, N: e3-g4, R: f5-e5, R: f5-d5, R: f5-c5, R: f5-f4, R: f5-f3, R: f5-f2, R: f5-f1, R: f5-f6, R: f5-g5, K: g1-f1, K: g1-f2, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h3, B: h5-g4, B: h5-f3, B: h5-e2, B: h5-d1, B: h5-g6, B: h5-f7, B: h5-e8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a relatively open position with several pieces developed. My king is on g1 (KWF1), having already moved, so castling is not possible. Black has a queen on c8, a rook on f8 and h7, knights on c6 and g8, and pawns scattered, with one on e5 that could be a threat if advanced. My material includes a knight on e3 (NWF1), bishop on h5 (BWF1), rook on f5 (RWF1), and a solid pawn structure on the queenside and center. Black’s king is on h4 (KBF1), which is unusually exposed — likely a critical vulnerability.\n\nBlack’s king on h4 is extremely exposed, sitting on an advanced file with little cover. This is highly unusual and dangerous for Black. The king has no immediate escape squares and is near the edge of the board. My pieces have potential to target this weakness.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **N: e3-g4**: This jumps the knight toward the enemy king, threatening check or pressure on f6/h6/e5. However, it doesn’t deliver immediate danger.\n- **B: h5-f7+**: This is a very strong candidate — the bishop can move from h5