osed files.\n\nMeanwhile, advancing pawns like g2-g4 or h2-h4 could support kingside play, but g2-g4 weakens the diagonal to g1 (Bg7-Bc3 ideas?), and h2-h4 is slow.\n\nBut **R: e5-h5** stands out as a direct, aggressive move that exploits the exposed black king.\n\nIs Rh5 safe? Can Black capture it? Only if something attacks h5 — currently, no black piece does. Knight on d4 is too far. Queen on b7 doesn’t cover h5. Pawn on g7 is gone. So yes, Rh5 is safe.\n\nTherefore, R: e5-h5 is both safe and threatening.\n\nReason: Moving the rook to h5 increases pressure on the kingside, directly targets the vulnerable black king on h6, and opens up tactical possibilities such as Rxh6+ or supporting a mating attack. It is more effective than slow pawn moves and better than shuffling the rook to inactive files.\n\nAnswer: R: e5-h5"}
