tend without support.\n- P: f3-f4 – Solid central expansion, gains space, supports e5 push or bishop/knight development. Safer than e4.\n- K: g1-f1, K: g1-f2, K: g1-g2, K: g1-h1 – King moves: all seem defensive. f1/f2/g2 move toward safety, but g2 is natural. However, moving the king now may waste time when better developing moves exist.\n- R: g7-f7, R: g7-e7, R: g7-g6, R: g7-g5, etc. – The rook on g7 is already active. Moving it back (f7, e7, g6) retreats. But R: g7-g5 threatens h5 or defends g2, though g5 may be met with ...h6. R: g7-g8+ would check the king, but Black’s king is on g8? Wait — no, KBF1 is on g8? Yes, KBF1 at g8. So R: g7-g8+ is a **discovered check** possibility? No, it\'s directly a rook check. R: g7-g8+ attacks the black king directly.\n\nWait — let\'s verify: RWF1 is on g7, KBF1 is on g8. So **R: g7-g8** is **checkmate candidate**? Let’s see:\n\n- After R: g7-g8, the white rook captures or checks on g8. Is the black king on g8? Yes. Is there any defender?\n\nLooking at the board:\n- Black king on g8\n- Black rook on h8 (RBF1)\n- But no other pieces near g8. Is g8 defended?\n\nCheck defenders of g8:\n- f7 has a black pawn (PBF0), yes — PBF0 on e7? Wait: PBF0 is on e7? No — current state shows:\n\nRow 7: |    |    |    |    |PBF0|    |RWF1|RBF1|\n\nSo e7: PBF0 (black pawn), f7: empty, g7: RWF1 (white rook), h7: RBF1 (black rook)\n\nRow 6: |    |    |    |    |PWF1|RBF1|    |KBF1|\n\nSo e6: PWF1 (white pawn), f6: RBF1 (black rook), g6: empty, h6: KBF1 (black king)\n\nWait — this contradicts earlier assumption.\n\nHold on — let\'s parse carefully.\n\nFrom row 6: \n“6 |    |    |    |    |PWF1|RBF1|    |KBF1| 6”\n\nSo:\n- e6: PWF1 (White Pawn)\n- f6: RBF1 (Black Rook)\n- g6: empty\n- h6: KBF1 → **Black King on h6?**\n\nThat can\'t be right — kings don\'t start on h6.\n\nWait — typo in parsing?\n\nLet me re-read:\n\nRow 8: all empty\n\nRow 7: a-d empty, e7: PBF0, f7: empty, g7: RWF1, h7: RBF1\n\nRow 6: a-d empty, e6: PWF1, f6: RBF1, g6: empty, h6: KBF1 → **King on h6?**\n\nBut that’s extremely unusual. Is this a misrepresentation?\n\nWait — perhaps it\'s g8: KBF1?\n\nNo — row 8 is all empty.\n\nThis suggests **Black king is on h6**, which is highly irregular — either a massive blunder or misplacement.\n\nBut per input:\n\n"6 |    |    |    |    |PWF1|RBF1|    |KBF1| 6"\n\nYes — h6: KBF1 → Black King on h6.\n\nAnd White king on g1.\n\nIs this legal? Possibly — maybe Black walked into danger.\n\nNow, evaluate threats.\n\nWhite rook on g7 — what does it attack?\n\ng7 → same file as h6 (king). Files: g and h are adjacent.\n\ng7 is on g-file, h6 is on h-file — so diagonal? g7 to h6 is one square diagonally up-right? No — from g7 to h6 is one rank down and one file right — so yes, **g7 to h6 is a diagonal move — but rooks don’t move diagonally**.\n\nRook moves orthogonally — same file or rank.\n\nRook on g7:\n- Same file: g8, g6, g5, g4, g3, g2, g1\n- Same rank: a7, b7, c7, d7, e7, f7, h7\n\nSo g7 rook attacks along g-file: g6, g5, ..., g1 and g8.\n\nBut h6 is not on g-file or 7th rank.\n\nSo rook on g7 does **not** attack h6.\n\nBut wait — is the black king really on h6?\n\nThat would be extremely exposed.\n\nCan White exploit this?\n\nLook at possible checks.\n\nWhat pieces can attack h6?\n\n- Queen? QBF1 is on d4 — not attacking h6.\n- Bishop? None placed.\n- Knight? None present.\n- Rook? Only if on h-file or 6th rank.\n\nWhite has rook on g7 — not on h or 6th.\n\nBut after **R: g7-g6**, the rook moves to g6 — which is same rank as h6.\n\nThen, **rook on g6 attacks h6 horizontally** — so **R: g7-g6 delivers check to the black king on h6**.\n\nAnd is it checkmate?\n\nAfter R: g7-g6+, Black king on h6 is in check.\n\nCan it move?\n- h6 → h5? Is h5 attacked? Not currently.\n- h6 → g5? Diagonally — but not a king move yet.\n\nBut more importantly: is h5 covered?\n\nCurrently, no white piece attacks h5.\n\nBut also, can Black block?\n\nThe check is horizontal — from g6 to h6.\n\nOnly way to block is to place a piece on