? After Rh7, no white piece attacks g6.\n\nSo king can go to g6, h5, or g5.\n\nBut **g6 is attacked by the rook on h7?** No — rook on h7 attacks h-file only.\n\nSo g6 is safe.\n\nThus, not checkmate.\n\nBut again, **free material gain and initiative**: we capture a rook and deliver check.\n\nMoreover, Black loses connection between rooks and exposes king.\n\nNow, compare to **R: g7-g6** — also attacks h6? No — g6 and h6 are adjacent files, different ranks.\n\ng6 and h6 are on same rank (6), adjacent files.\n\nSo rook on g6 attacks along rank 6 — so h6 is within range.\n\nYes! **R: g7-g6** moves to g6, then attacks h6 horizontally — so **check**.\n\nAnd again, king must move.\n\nBut **no capture** — we don’t take the rook on h7.\n\nWhereas **R: g7-h7** captures the rook and gives check.\n\nSo **R: g7-h7 is superior** — gains material and tempo.\n\nBut is it safe?\n\nAfter Rxh7+, Black plays ...Kxh7 — king captures the rook.\n\nThen we lose the rook, but **we captured a rook and gave check, and they captured back** — so even material? We had rook, they had rook — trade.\n\nBut their king is still exposed.\n\nAnd we have other threats.\n\nBut is there a better follow-up?\n\nAlternatively, **P: c5-c6** — breaks in center, could open lines.\n\nBut less urgent than exploiting the exposed king.\n\nNow, **why is Black’s king on h6**? Likely a blunder — perhaps they played Kh6? But why?\n\nPossibly escaping check or misplaced.\n\nRegardless, it’s our turn — we must punish it.\n\nBest move: **R: g7-h7** — captures rook and checks king.\n\nBut let\'s confirm: is h7 really occupied by Black rook? Yes — RBF1.\n\nAnd move list includes **R: g7-h7** — so it\'s allowed.\n\nAfter Rxh7+, Black must respond to check.\n\nOptions:\n- ...Kxh7 — king captures\n- ...Kh5 — runs\n- ...g6 — blocks? g6 is empty — yes, but rook attacks along file — from h7 to h6, so g6 is not on the line. Blocking requires same line — so only h-file blockers: g6 is not on h-file.\n\nTo block a vertical check on