\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1|    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b3-b4, P: c7-c8-q, P: c7-c8-n, P: c7-c8-b, P: c7-c8-r, K: f1-f2, P: f3-f4, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and currently have a somewhat passive position. My king is on f1 (KWF1), having already moved, so castling is not possible. Black has a rook on e6 (RBF1) and queen on g6 (QBF1), both active, and their king is on h5 (KBF1), which is unusually far forward — possibly a blunder or trap. Black also has a pawn on e7 (PBF0) that hasn't moved yet. My pawns are scattered, with potential to advance. The most immediate tactical opportunity is the promotion of the c7-pawn, which is one square away from queening.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: c7-c8-q – Promotes to queen immediately, gaining a powerful piece deep in enemy territory. This puts immense pressure on Black, especially since their king is exposed on h5. A queen on c8 can potentially threaten along the back rank or support attacks.\n- P: c7-c8-n/r/b – Under-promotions are less valuable here; knight might fork, but no immediate tactical follow-up is evident. Rook or bishop underpromotion offers no benefit over queen.\n- P: b3-b4 – Prepares to support c7-c8 or advance further, but delays immediate material gain.\n- P: f3-f4 – Gains space in the center, but doesn’t capitalize on the urgent chance to promote.\n- P: h2-h3/h2-h4 – Weakly advances a flank pawn, offering little strategic or tactical value.\n- K: f1-f2 – Centralizes the king slightly, but king safety isn’t critical now, and this wastes tempo when promotion is available.\n\nReason: The most forcing and advantageous move is promoting the c7-pawn to a queen. It gains significant material and initiative. With Black’s king on h5, the new queen on c8 could rapidly join an attack via moves like Qc8-h3+ or support other threats. Delaying promotion risks allowing Black counterplay.\n\nAnswer: P: c7-c8-q"}
