moves to g4 — that square is occupied by the enemy king. This is only allowed if it\'s a capture, and it would end the game (checkmate) if the king cannot escape.\n\nBut can the king escape? From g4, possible escapes: f3, f5, h3, h5, g5, f4, h4 — standard king moves.\n\nAfter Qe6-g4+, the king is attacked on g4. Where can it go?\n- f3: is it attacked? Queen on g4 controls f3? g4 to f3 is one left, one down — yes, diagonal. So f3 is attacked.\n- f4: one left, same rank — horizontal? Queen controls f4? g4 to f4 is one left — yes, same rank. So f4 is attacked.\n- f5: one left, one up — diagonal — yes, attacked.\n- g5: one up — same file — yes, attacked.\n- h5: one right, one up — diagonal — yes, attacked.\n- h4: one right — same rank — yes, attacked.\n- h3: one right, one down — diagonal — yes, attacked.\n\nSo all eight surrounding squares are under attack by the queen on g4.\n\nBut can the king stay? No — it\'s in check.\n\nCan it be captured? Yes — the move Qe6-g4 gives check, and since all escape squares are controlled by the queen, it\'s **checkmate**.\n\nBut wait — is the square g4 protected by another black piece? Yes — the black queen is on g3, which is adjacent to g4. So if White plays Qxg4, Black can recapture Qxg4. But that would mean the white queen is captured, and the king is still under threat?\n\nNo — when the white queen moves to g4, it delivers check to the black king. The black king must respond. But all escape squares are attacked by the queen. Even though the black queen can recapture, **that does not remove the check**, because the king is still in check — you cannot recapture *after* being in check; you must get out of check first.\n\nIn chess, you cannot make a move that leaves your king in check. So if the white queen moves to g4, the black king is in check from the queen. The black queen on g3 can capture the white queen on g4, but after that capture, the black king would still be in check? No — after Qxg4 (capture), the white queen is gone, and the black queen is on g4. The black king is still on g4? No — the king was on g4, and the white queen moved to g4 — that’s impossible unless it\'s a capture.\n\nClarification: When a piece moves to a square occupied by an enemy piece, it captures it. But the king cannot be captured — instead, the game ends when the king is in check and cannot escape.\n\nSo: if White plays Qe6-g4, the queen attacks the king on g4. Since the king is now in check, and all its surrounding squares are attacked (f3, f4, f5, g5, h5, h4, h3, f3), and there is no piece that can interpose (queen moving diagonally/horizontally/vertically, but the only line is from e6 to g4 — passing through f5? e6-f5-g4. Is f5 occupied? No — empty. So could a piece interpose? But the king is in direct line — but the queen is moving directly to g4, landing on the king\'s square? No — you don\'t land on the king.\n\nThis is a confusion in representation.\n\nIn actual chess notation, you don\'t move to the king\'s square. Instead, you move to the square where the king is, which results in check (if it attacks it), but you don\'t physically replace it. The move Qg4+ means the queen moves to g4, and if the king is there, it\'s illegal unless it\'s a capture — but again, you don\'t capture the king.\n\nThe correct understanding: **The king is on g4. The queen moves to g4 — this is only possible if it captures the king, which ends the game. But this is only allowed if the king is not protected.**\n\nBut in reality, the move is legal **only if it delivers check and there is no defense**.\n\nLet’s simulate:\n\n- Current position: Black king on g4, White queen on e6.\n- White plays Qg4+ (e6-g4).\n- Now, the queen is on g4, attacking the king on g4 — but they are on the same square? No — the king is on g4, and the queen moves to g4 — that would require capturing the king.\n- In chess, you cannot make a move that places your piece on the same square as your opponent\'s king unless it\'s a capture, and it results in checkmate if the king cannot escape.\n\nBut here’s the key: **the square g4 is occupied by the black king. Therefore, the white queen cannot move there unless it captures the king — which is only allowed if it\'s checkmate.**\n\nAnd is it checkmate? After Qe6-g4, the black king is attacked on g4. Can it move? All adjacent squares:\n- f3: threatened by queen? g4 to f3 is diagonal — yes.\n- f4: horizontal — yes.\n- f5: diagonal — yes.\n- g5: vertical — yes.\n- h5: diagonal — yes.\n- h4: horizontal — yes.\n- h3: diagonal — yes.\n- g3: vertical — yes? g4 to g3 is down one — yes, same file.\n\nWait — g3 is occupied by the black queen (QBF1). So the king cannot move to g3 anyway — occupied.\n\nBut more importantly: **is the king in check after the queen moves to g4?** No — because the queen cannot move to a square occupied by the king unless it\'s a capture, and in standard rules, such a move is represented as Qxg4+, and it is legal only if it\'s check and there is no defense.\n\nBut crucially, **can the black queen on g3 capture the white queen on g4?** Only if the white queen moves to g4. But after the white queen moves to g4, the black king is in check. The black player must respond by either:\n1. Moving the king — but all squares are attacked.\n2. Capturing the checking piece — yes, with Qg3xg4.\n\nBut after Qg3xg4, the black queen is on g4, and the black king is still on g4? No — the king hasn\'t moved. Two pieces cannot occupy the same square.\n\nSo when the black queen captures on g4, it replaces the white queen, but the black king is still on g4 — so both the black queen and king would be on g4? Impossible.\n\nAh — here’s the flaw: **you cannot have two pieces on the same square.**\n\nTherefore, for the white queen to move to g4, the black king must not be there — but it is. So the only way the move is legal is if it captures the king — which ends the game immediately upon checkmate.\n\nBut in practice, the move Qe6-g4 is a **discovered check**? No.\n\nLet’s reevaluate: **The black king is on g4. The white queen is on e6. The queen can move along the diagonal to g4, but that square is occupied by the king. In chess notation, this is written as Qxg4, and it is a legal move only if it results in check and the king cannot be saved.**\n\nBut after Qxg4, the king is attacked. Can it move? As above, all eight surrounding squares are under control of the queen on g4. So no escape.\n\nCan a piece capture the queen? Yes — the black queen on g3 can play Qxg4, but after that, the black queen is on g4, and the black king is still on g4 — impossible.\n\nNo — when the black queen captures on g4, it moves to g4, but the black king is already there — so it would be capturing its own king? No — the king is not captured. The act of capturing the white queen on g4 by the black queen would require the black queen to move to g4, which is occupied by the black king — so it\'s an illegal move.\n\n**Yes!** A piece cannot move to a square occupied by its own king. Therefore, the black queen on g3 cannot capture the white queen on g4 because g4 is occupied by the black king.\n\nTherefore, **the black queen cannot recapture** — because it would have to move to g4, which is occupied by its own king.\n\nThus, after Qe6-g4+, the black king is in check, has no legal moves (all eight directions are attacked), and no piece can interpose or capture the checking piece (because the only potential capturer, the black queen, cannot move to g4 due to self-occupation).\n\nTherefore, **Qe6-g4 is checkmate**.\n\nMoreover, the move "Q: e6-g4" is in the valid move list.\n\nOther moves like Q: e6-e4 or P: b3-b4 do not lead to immediate mate.\n\nTherefore, this is the winning move.\n\nReason: [Playing Q: e6-g4 delivers checkmate. The black king on g4 is attacked by the white queen, all escape squares are controlled, and the black queen on g3 cannot capture the queen because g4 is occupied by the black king itself, making the capture illegal. No other piece can interpose or 