\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |KWF1|    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b3-a4, P: b3-b4, K: f1-e1, K: f1-e2, K: f1-f2, K: f1-g1, K: f1-g2, P: g4-g5, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the board is in a relatively open position with few pieces remaining. My king (KWF1) is on f1, slightly exposed but not currently in check. Black has a pawn on e7 (PBF0), another on g4 (PBF1), and a king on h4 (KBF1). My own pawns are on b3 (PWF1), g4 (PWF1), and h2 (PWF0). The most immediate threats involve potential advancement of pawns toward promotion, particularly my b3 and g4 pawns, while also being cautious of the black king potentially entering my territory.\n\nKey observations:\n- Black's king is on h4 — very close to my g4 and h2 pawns, which could become weaknesses if not advanced carefully.\n- My pawn on g4 is already advanced but could be captured by the black king unless supported or pushed.\n- My pawn on b3 has two options: a4 or b4. Advancing it may help create a passed pawn.\n- The black pawn on e7 is poised to advance and could become dangerous if not monitored.\n- King activity for me is limited; Kf1 can move to e1, e2, f2, g1, or g2 — mostly for safety or coordination.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: b3-a4 — Gains space on the queenside, but doesn’t create an immediate threat. Somewhat passive.\n- P: b3-b4 — Creates a strong pawn chain push, potentially opening lines. Could support a future c4 or prepare for b5 push. More dynamic than a4.\n- P: g4-g5 — Pushes the kingside pawn forward, challenging the black king’s proximity. This restricts Kh4 from capturing on g4 and aims to promote. High value due to proximity to promotion square (g8).\n- K: f1-e1 / e2 / f2 / g1 / g2 — All king moves are defensive or preparatory. None improve my winning chances significantly at this stage; best used when needed for defense.\n- P: h2-h3 — A waiting move that prevents ...g4-g3 ideas by Black, but doesn't contribute much offensively.\n\nAmong these, P: g4-g5 stands out because:\n- It advances a pawn closer to promotion (just 3 more moves away from g8).\n- It restricts the black king from easily approaching via g4 or h5.\n- It gains space and initiative on the kingside where the enemy king is located.\n- Delaying this push risks allowing Black to capture on g4 or gain counterplay.\n\nIn contrast, pushing the b-pawn is less urgent since there’s no immediate breakthrough, and king moves do not increase pressure.\n\nReason: The key to winning such endgame-like positions is creating a passed pawn and promoting it before the opponent. The g4-g5 push increases pressure on the kingside, limits Black’s king mobility, and brings me closer to a potential queening square. This proactive move maximizes my winning chances compared to passive or slow alternatives.\n\nAnswer: P: g4-g5"}
