lower.\n\nBlack’s king is on g5, very close to my h2 pawn — it could capture it soon if not advanced. The h-pawn is still on h2 (PWF0), so it can move two squares. Advancing it now prevents loss and starts creating a potential passed pawn on the kingside, which could be critical in this endgame.\n\nReason: In endgames with few pieces, king activity and pawn promotion races are decisive. My king is passive, but advancing the h-pawn immediately with h2-h4 creates a threat of h4-h5-h6-h7-h8, promoting to a queen. This forces Black’s king to spend tempi dealing with it. Doing nothing risks losing the h-pawn for free. Among all moves, h2-h4 is the most proactive and creates immediate strategic pressure.\n\nAnswer: P: h2-h4"}
