 the queenside pawns. More active.\n- K: f1-f2 — Moves king up, supporting the h-pawn and preparing to escort it. Also defends g2 and h2.\n- K: f1-g1 — Slightly awkward; doesn’t help much. No clear plan.\n- K: f1-g2 — Good square! Activates the king toward the center and supports both the h-pawn and potential b-pawn advance. Very useful.\n- P: h4-h5 — Pushes the h-pawn, but it's isolated and far from support. Black king can easily block or chase it. Risky without king support.\n\nBest options are either advancing the b-pawn to start creating a passed pawn or activating the king to support promotion.\n\nReason: The most effective way to win in endgames like this is to create a passed pawn and push it with king support. The b3-b4 move starts building a passer, and since the b-pawn is supported by the b2 pawn, it can become strong. Alternatively, king activation with K: f1-g2 prepares to support both wings. However, **P: b3-b4** creates immediate central tension and initiative. It prevents Black from ignoring the queenside. Compared to slow king moves or risky pawn pushes, this is the most constructive and forcing winning attempt.\n\nAnswer: P: b3-b4"}
