n on the h-file. Since the black king is on g4, advancing h4-h5 gains space and pushes the king back.\n- King moves: All revolve around e2. Moving the king toward the center or to support pawns is useful in endgames. However, moving away from the center might cede control. Key options:\n  - K: e2-e3 – Centralizes slightly and supports h4-h5 push by preparing king escort.\n  - K: e2-f2 – Moves toward the h-pawn, preparing to support its promotion.\n  - K: e2-d2 – Retreats slightly, less active.\n  - K: e2-d1/e1/f1 – These retreat to the back rank and seem passive.\n\nBest ideas: In king and pawn endgames, the key principles are:\n1. Activate the king.\n2. Create passed pawns.\n3. Prevent enemy king from accessing key squares.\n\nThe h-pawn is closer to queening than the b-pawn. Pushing h4-h5 gains space and forces the black king to deal with it. Alternatively, preparing with king movement (like Kf2 or Ke3) could be better—but h4-h5 is immediately useful.\n\nHowever, note that after h4-h5, Black’s king may have to retreat, and I can follow up with Kf3 or Kf2 to support the pawn.\n\nBut also consider: if I don’t push h4-h5 now, Black may maneuver to capture the h-pawn. So immediate push is valuable.\n\nb4-b5 is less urgent—it creates a queenside pawn, but it's far from promotion and Black can potentially ignore it.\n\nKing moves like e2-f2 or e2-e3 are good for activation, but since h4-h5 is a tempo-gaining move (attacking space), it should be prioritized.\n\nReason: Pushing the h-pawn gains space, limits the black king, and begins creating a potential promotion threat. It uses a pawn move when the king is still positioned to support it. Delaying it may allow Black to improve their king position. Therefore, h4-h5 is the most proactive and strategically sound choice.\n\nAnswer: P: h4-h5"}
