owed), but Black may avoid with king moves, though here KBF1 is on g1, so after Qf7+, Black king must go to h1. Then Qf1# again threatens.\n- **Q: e7-h7** – Checks: Qh7+! Kg2? Not possible — king is on g1, so Kh1 only. So Qh7+ forces Kh1, then Qh2# next move — also a potential mate!\n\nWait: Let's verify:\n\nAfter **Q: e7-h7+**, Black king on g1 must go to h1 (Kh1). Then White plays Qh2#, since h2 is supported and all escapes are blocked.\n\nSimilarly, **Q: e7-f7+** also forces Kh1, then Qf1#.\n\nBut **Q: e7-f8** is not check — f8 does not attack g1. So no.\n\nBest idea: Deliver a check that forces Kh1, then follow up with a back-rank mate.\n\nSo checking moves like **Q: e7-h7+**, **Q: e7-f7+**, **Q: e7-g7+**, **Q: e7-d7+** all force Kh1.\n\nAmong these, **Q: e7-h7+** is most effective because:\n- It forces Kh1\n- Then Qh2# is unavoidable unless Black has defense — but they don’t.\n- After Qh7+, if Kh1, then next move Qh2#.\n\nAlternatively, **Q: e7-f7+** also works, leading to Qf1#.\n\nBut note: **Q: e7-f7** is a valid move and delivers check.\n\nAlso, **Q: e7-g7+** would work too, but not in the list.\n\nFrom the list, **Q: e7-f7**, **Q: e7-d7**, **Q: e7-h7**, **Q: e7-e8**, etc., are options.\n\nBut only those that give check are best.\n\nSo strongest moves:\n- **Q: e7-h7** – gives check, forces Kh1, sets up Qh2#\n- **Q: e7-f7** – gives check, forces Kh1, sets up Qf1#\n\nBoth lead to forced mate in two.\n\nBut **Q: e7-h7** is better because h-file is more aligned with the king’s position, and fewer interferences.\n\nAlso, **Q: e7-h7** is listed.\n\nReason: The fastest path to checkmate is to force the black king to h1 with a queen check along the 7th rank, then deliver mate on h2. **Q: e7-h7+** achieves this, and since no other pieces can interfere, it leads to forced checkmate. Among the valid options, this is one of the most direct.\n\nAnswer: Q: e7-h7"}
