\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b4-b5, K: e2-d2, K: e2-e1, K: e2-e3, Q: h7-g6, Q: h7-f5, Q: h7-e4, Q: h7-d3, Q: h7-c2, Q: h7-g7, Q: h7-f7, Q: h7-e7, Q: h7-d7, Q: h7-c7, Q: h7-b7, Q: h7-a7, Q: h7-g8, Q: h7-h6, Q: h7-h5, Q: h7-h4, Q: h7-h3, Q: h7-h2, Q: h7-h1, Q: h7-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and currently have a queen on h7, a pawn on b4, and my king on e2. The black king is on g2 (KBF1), which is extremely close to the edge of the board. My queen is poised to deliver a strong attack, and there is an immediate opportunity to threaten checkmate or win material. The black king has limited mobility and is exposed.\n\nEvaluation:\n- Q: h7-g6 – Attacks g2 directly; checks the black king. Black would have to respond immediately.\n- Q: h7-f5 – Controls key central squares but does not give check.\n- Q: h7-e4 – Puts pressure on the center and eyes the weak g2 square, but not forcing.\n- Q: h7-c2 – Delivers check to the black king at g2? Let's verify: c2 attacks along the diagonal — c2 to g6 is the line, but g2 is not on that diagonal. So no check.\n- Q: h7-g8 – Moves away from action; passive.\n- Q: h7-d3 – No immediate threat.\n- Q: h7-h6 – Prepares to potentially support a pawn push or restrict the king, but not urgent.\n- Q: h7-g7 – Closes in on g2: g7 attacks h6, f6, etc., but not a direct check.\n- Q: h7-g6 – This move attacks g2 directly: queen on g6 checks the king on g2 with no pieces blocking. After Qg6+, black must respond with Kf2 or Kh2. Either way, the black king is driven into a worse position, and we maintain initiative.\n\nWait: Is g2 actually occupied by the black king? Yes — KBF1 is on g2 per the board. So any queen move that lands on the same rank, file, or diagonal attacking g2 is a check.\n\nNow check if Qg6 attacks g2:  \nYes — both are on the g-file. Queen on g6 attacks g2 directly (vertical). No pieces between. So Q: h7-g6 delivers check.\n\nMoreover, this is a very strong move because:\n- It forces the black king to move (only legal moves likely are Kh2 or Kf2).\n- It activates the most powerful piece into a dominant position.\n- It may lead to a quick mating net, especially since Black has no visible queenside or kingside defenders.\n\nOther queen moves like Qh7-h1 or Qh7-c2 might look tempting for back-rank threats, but after Qh7-c2, the diagonal doesn't hit g2 (c2-g2 is not diagonal: c2 to g6 is Bxf6 idea, not relevant). So only vertical/horizontal/diagonal lines matter.\n\nQ: h7-c7? That’s horizontal — same rank? No, h7 and c7 are both on 7th rank — yes! So Q: h7-c7 moves the queen across the 7th rank.\n\nBut more importantly: Q: h7-g6 is check and highly forcing.\n\nReason: The most effective move is to deliver check with the queen via Q: h7-g6, pressuring the exposed black king on g2. This limits Black’s responses and increases winning chances through immediate aggression. Delaying check allows Black to potentially improve their position.\n\nAnswer: Q: h7-g6"}
