6-f7** – Controls g8, h6, and checks from a distance. Doesn’t directly attack h2, but supports a mating net.\n- **Q: g6-c6** or other distant queen moves – Too passive; allow black king to potentially wander, wasting time.\n- **P: b4-b5** – A quiet pawn move, irrelevant in this immediate mating context.\n- **K: e2-e3/f2/d2/etc** – King movement is too slow; the queen should deliver mate without needing king support.\n\nBest line: **Q: g6-g7** delivers check and forces the black king to h1 (its only legal move, assuming no other pieces). Then, Qg7-h7 delivers checkmate, as the king cannot escape and has no defenders.\n\nAlternatively, after Qg6-g7+ Kh1, if I don’t have immediate mate, I can follow up with precise queen checks.\n\nBut wait — is **Q: g6-h7** possible now? No, because h7 is not in the valid move list. Looking at the valid moves, **Q: g6-g7** *is* available.\n\nAlso, **Q: g6-f7** is available — f7 attacks g8, h7, and h5, but does not check h2.\n\nOnly **Q: g6-g7** and **Q: g6-h6** directly threaten the king.\n\nWait: From g6, queen to g7 attacks h7, g8, f7, etc., but does it attack h2?\n\nNo! g7 is not on the same rank, file, or diagonal as h2. g7 to h2 is a knight move — not aligned.\n\nSo **Q: g6-g7** does NOT attack h2.\n\nLet’s reevaluate alignment:\n\nBlack king is on h2.\n\nQueen on g6:\n\n- Same diagonal? h2 = h2, g6 = g6. Difference: file -1, rank +4 → not on same diagonal.\n- Same file? No.\n- Same rank? No.\n\nCan queen reach h2 from g6? Only if moving along diagonal: g6 → h5 → h2? No, that’s not straight.\n\nActually, g6 to h2 is not a valid queen path. The queen cannot attack h2 from g6.\n\nBut wait — what squares does the queen on g6 control?\n\nFrom g6, queen can move:\n- Horizontally: entire 6th rank (g6-a6, g6-h6)\n- Vertically: g-file from g1 to g8\n- Diagonals: \n   - a1–h8 diagonal: a1, b2, c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8\n   - a8–h1 diagonal: h5, f7, e8 — so g6 is not on a8–h1\n\nWait: g6 is on the diagonal from a1 to h8? Let\'s see:\na1, b2, c3, d4, e5, f6, g7, h8 — so g7 is on that diagonal, not g6.\n\ng6 is on the diagonal: b1–h7? b1, c2, d3, e4, f5, g6, h7 → yes!\n\nSo queen on g6 controls h7 via diagonal (g6 → h7).\n\nAlso, queen controls g-file (g1–g8) and 6th rank (a6–h6).\n\nNow, black king on h2:\n- Can it be attacked by queen on g6?\n\nCheck possible attack paths:\n- Along file: queen would need to be on h-file → not.\n- Along rank: queen on 2nd rank → not.\n- Diagonal: Is there a diagonal from g6 to h2?\n\nSlope: from g6 to h2: +1 file, -4 rank → not 45-degree diagonal → no.\n\nSo **queen on g6 does NOT attack h2**.\n\nBut then why is black not already in check? Because he isn\'t.\n\nBut more importantly — is the black king under threat?\n\nCurrently, black king on h2 has possible moves: h1, h3, g1, g2, g3.\n\nQueen on g6 controls:\n- Entire g-file: so g1, g2, g3, g4, g5, g6, g7, g8 are all controlled.\n→ So g1, g2, g3 are attacked by queen → black king cannot go to g1, g2, or g3.\n\nSimilarly, queen controls 6th rank → h6, but not h2.\n\nAnd diagonally: g6 is on diagonal c2–h7 → c2, d3, e4, f5, g6, h7.\n\nSo queen attacks h7.\n\nTherefore, black king on h2 can only move to: h1, h3 (since g1/g2/g3 are attacked).\n\nBut h3 is on g-file? No, h3 is h3 → not on g-file → not attacked by queen unless on diagonal or same rank.\n\nIs h3 on the same diagonal as g6? g6 to h3: +1 file, -3 rank → no.\n\nSame rank? No.\n\nSame file? No.\n\nSo h3 is **not** attacked by queen on g6.\n\nSimilarly, h1 is not attacked.\n\nSo black king can legally move to h1 and h3.\n\nBut wait — is h1 safe? h1 is not attacked now.\n\nBut if I move queen to h6, then h6 attacks h1–h8 file.\n\nAh! That’s the key.\n\nIf I play **Q: g6-h6**, then the queen occupies the h-file, attacking h1, h2, h3, etc.\n\nCurrently, black king is on h2 — so if I play Qh6, that would be **check**, because h6 and h2 are on same file.\n\nYes! h-file.\n\nSo **Q: g6-h6** gives check to the king on h2.\n\nAfter Qh6+, black king must move to h1 or h3.\n\