lemate.\n- K: d4-d5 — Central advancement. Keeps flexibility. Black king still free.\n- K: d4-e3 — This moves to e3. Then Black king on c2 would have moves to b1, b2, b3, c3, d3, d2, d1 — all fine. But e3 is closer to centralizing.\n- K: d4-e4 — Central control. Strong in endgames, but with no other pieces, it doesn’t matter much.\n- K: d4-e5 — Further up, but less connected to Black’s king. Might allow escape.\n\nBut crucially: Is any move causing stalemate?\n\nAfter K: d4-e3, Black king on c2 has moves to b1, b2, b3, c3, d3, d2, d1 — all exist. Not stalemate.\n\nWait — what is Black\'s current position? c2. From c2, legal moves are: b1, b2, b3, c3, c1, d1, d2, d3 — assuming no obstruction. All these squares are empty. So Black has many moves.\n\nThus, none of the moves immediately cause stalemate. But we want to **centralize** and **approach** the Black king to limit its space over time.\n\nBest plan: Bring White king closer to Black king to reduce mobility.\n\nd4 to e3 brings White king within striking distance: from e3, White can go to d2, e2, f2, etc., to challenge.\n\nBut c4 also approaches: c4 is adjacent to c3, b3, b4, b5, c5, d5, d4, d3 — so it threatens to cut off Black.\n\nActually, **K: d4-c4** places the White king on c4, which is only one square away from c2 (via c3). It prepares to advance toward b3 or d3 later.\n\nSimilarly, **K: d4-e3** puts the king on e3, which is farther from c2 (distance: e3 to c2 is knight-move apart), so less direct.\n\nClosest approach is via c4 → c3 → c2 or b3.\n\nSo **K: d4-c4** is better because it directly pressures the file where Black king resides.\n\nBut wait — after K: d4-c4, Black plays ...Kc2-d3? Or ...Kb2? Still not forced.\n\nBut since no pawns exist, this game is drawn regardless. Our best "winning" chance is if Black makes an illegal move or disconnects — but that\'s not chess.\n\nTherefore, the goal is to make the most principled move.\n\nIn king vs king endgames, the idea is to outflank and centralize.\n\nHowever, c4 is on the same file as d4 was — just shifted left.\n\ne4 or e3 may help control more central squares.\n\nBut Black is on c2 — so the action is on the queenside.\n\nThus, moving toward c4 or b4 makes sense.\n\nAmong the options, **K: d4-c4** is the closest step toward opposing the Black king.\n\nAlso, **K: d4-c5** goes to c5, which is further from c2 than c4 is.\n\nc4 is rank 4, c2 is rank 2 — same file almost.\n\nDistance from d4 to c2: 2.5 squares (king-move: d4–c3–c2).\n\nFrom c4: c4–c3–c2: two moves.\n\nSame as before.\n\nBut c4 controls c3, which is key.\n\nIf White king goes to c4, Black cannot go to c3.\n\nIt reduces Black’s mobility slightly.\n\nSimilarly, e3 does not threaten any of Black’s squares.\n\nSo **K: d4-c4** is better.\n\nBut let\'s see: after K: d4-c4, Black plays ...Kc2-d3? Then White can play Kc4-d5, chasing.\n\nAlternatively, if Black goes to b2, White can go Kb4.\n\nSo yes, c4 is good.\n\nBut what about K: d4-e3? That allows Ke3-d2, then threatening c3.\n\nBut d2 is closer to c2.\n\ne3 to d2 is one move.\n\nBut from d4 to e3 is going away from the center of activity.\n\nBlack is on c2 — queenside.\n\nWhite king on d4 is centralized.\n\nMoving to e3 or e4 drifts to the kingside — wrong direction.\n\nSo moves like e3, e4, e5 are suboptimal.\n\nSimilarly, d5 is forward but not toward the enemy king.\n\nSo best moves are c4 or c5.\n\nc4 is closer to c2 than c5.\n\nc4 is only 2 king moves from c2; c5 is 3.\n\nSo c4 is better.\n\nAlso, c4 prepares support for potential b-file advances (if there were pawns).\n\nBut there are none.\n\nStill, **K: d4-c4** is the most direct.\n\nIs there a risk of stalemate?\n\nNo. After Kc4, Black has Kb2, Kc1, Kc3, Kd1, Kd2, Kd3, Bb1, Bb3, Bd3 — plenty of moves.\n\nNo stalemate.\n\nSo safe.\n\nReason: Moving the king to c4 brings it closer to Black\'s king on c2, increases control over key central and queenside squares, and begins to restrict Black\'s king mobility. It is the most direct and principled move in a king vs king endgame, aiming to dominate the center and approach the opponent.\n\nAnswer: K: d4-c4'}
